  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1224 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nIsolated or widely scattered showers are possible on Saturday, \nmainly in the morning, as a minor impulse aloft moves east-northeast \ninto Texas and the southern Plains. Temperatures will be warmer \nSaturday (highs in the lower to mid 70s) but considerable cloud \ncover is expected to persist, given the southerly low-level flow and \nample low-level moisture. The warming trend will continue Sunday as \na shortwave upper ridge moves east over Texas. May see some breaks \nin the clouds in the afternoon, but skies overall are expected to \nstay mostly cloudy. Expect highs to be the upper 70s to lower 80s in \nour northern and central counties, and mid to upper 70s farther \nsouth. \n\nA strong system will affect our area early next week. A large scale \nupper low and trough will enter the western CONUS Sunday, and \noverspread the Rockies on Monday. A low is progged to develop in the \nsouthern portion of this trough over northern New Mexico, and then \ntrack across the Texas Panhandle into western Oklahoma Monday night. \nCold front associated with this system is forecast to move southeast \nacross our area in the Monday evening/Monday night time frame.\nThere is still uncertainty with the details in the eventual track of \nthe upper low. But appears at this time that the track of the upper \nlow and trough will be far enough south with sufficient moisture for \na band of showers and thunderstorms to develop along the cold front, \nas it pushes across our area. The higher rain chances and better \ncoverage of convection look to be in our eastern and southeastern \ncounties. \n\nCooler air will follow this frontal passage and will be accompanied \nby breezy northwest winds on Tuesday. After a warm day Monday with \nhighs in the upper 70s to mid 80s, highs Tuesday will be in the \nlower to mid 70s. May see some wrap around cloud cover over the \nnortheast part of our area on Tuesday, as moisture wraps around the \ndeparting upper system. Dry weather to follow for Wednesday and \nThursday, as surface high pressure settles into Texas by Wednesday \nand shifts to the east on Thursday. After the period of well-above \nnormal morning low temperatures our area has seen in the past \nseveral days, will have a couple of cool nights Tuesday night and \nWednesday night with lows mostly in the 40s. The coolest night looks \nto be Wednesday night when better radiational cooling could allow \nfor temperatures to dip into the upper 30s to lower 40s at some \nlocations.",
